From 75bfedda091e8eb242868783fee6b569f105ae1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Durham <amdnative@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:14:33 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] hyundai: fuel level from the KDMLink-validated CLU13 sender
 word -> CarStateSP.fuelLevel

The KDMLink open standard (github.com/531dev/kdmlink; folded into the DBC by patch 0046) decodes
the 16-bit LE fuel-sender word at CLU13 (0x50C) bits 24-39: pct = raw * 0.30201 - 612.17. It
replaces the 0x5CD D0 read (a drive-frozen non-fuel byte) and coexists with the legacy 10-bit
CF_Clu_DTE interpretation of the same bits.

Verified as a real sender against the owner's routes (519 segments): 0.302 %/count steps,
monotonic decrease, continuous across drives (24.8 -> 21.4 % over routes 154-155; refuel;
97.3 -> 95.1 -> 91.2 % over 156-157). Two bus transients were characterized: a ~6 s out-of-band
start burst (+1258..+1334 %, some ignitions) and a single end-of-drive zero-payload frame
(raw 2048 = +6.3 %, IN band; runs of at most 3 frames). fuel_level.py drops out-of-band frames
and its FuelLevelTracker holds the last good value across CAN gaps and requires
FUEL_CONFIRM_WINDOWS sightings before accepting a > FUEL_STEP_MAX jump, so neither transient can
move the published level.

Published on CarStateSP.fuelLevel (coupled with custom.capnp - see the superproject half):
percent, 0.0 = no valid level accepted yet. Read path: CarStateExt.update_fuel_level reads
vl_all off the already-subscribed CLU13 powertrain-parser message; no other signal is touched.
